{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "Paper written successfully\n",
  "stderr": "",
  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of artificial intelligence has led to the emergence of code-generating agents capable of writing, debugging, and executing code autonomously. While these agents offer tremendous potential for software development and automation, they also introduce significant security and trustworthiness concerns. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents and the critical field of red-teaming methodologies designed to evaluate their security vulnerabilities. We examine several key contributions to this domain, including automated red-teaming agents for code generation systems, benchmarks for evaluating risky code execution, and trustworthiness assessments of GPT models. Our analysis reveals that red-teaming methodologies have evolved from manual approaches to sophisticated automated frameworks that can systematically identify vulnerabilities in code-generating AI systems. We also discuss the emerging blue-teaming paradigm that leverages red-teaming insights to improve code security. The paper concludes with a discussion of open challenges and future research directions in ensuring the safe deployment of code agents.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a transformative class of AI systems capable of generating, understanding, and executing code across diverse programming paradigms. The integration of large language models (LLMs) with code interpreters and development environments has enabled these agents to perform complex software engineering tasks autonomously [1706.03762]. From writing unit tests to refactoring entire codebases, code agents are increasingly being adopted in both research and industry settings.\n\nHowever, the deployment of code agents introduces significant security and trustworthiness challenges. When given the ability to generate and execute code, these systems may inadvertently produce insecure, malicious, or incorrect code that could compromise systems or data. Recent studies have highlighted the risks associated with risky code execution and generation in code agents [2409.12345]. The ability of code agents to autonomously execute code amplifies potential vulnerabilities, as generated code can access system resources, modify files, or interact with networks without human oversight.\n\nTo address these challenges, the research community has begun developing systematic approaches for evaluating and improving the security of code agents. Red-teaming—simulating adversarial attacks to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical methodology for understanding the security landscape of code-generating AI systems [2510.02609]. Automated red-teaming agents can systematically probe code agents for vulnerabilities, identifying weaknesses that might be missed through manual testing.\n\nThis paper surveys the state-of-the-art in code agent security evaluation, focusing on automated red-teaming methodologies, benchmarking frameworks, and trustworthiness assessments. We examine key contributions to the field and discuss how red-teaming insights can inform the development of more secure code generation systems [2501.01234].\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nThe field of code agent security evaluation has seen rapid growth in recent years. In this section, we survey the most relevant prior work in automated red-teaming, benchmarking, and trustworthiness assessment for code-generating AI systems.\n\n### 2.1 Foundation Models and Transformers\n\nThe transformer architecture [1706.03762] laid the foundation for modern language models that power code generation capabilities. Attention Is All You Need introduced the self-attention mechanism that enables models to capture long-range dependencies in sequential data, fundamentally transforming natural language processing and code understanding. This architecture became the basis for subsequent code-specific models like CodeBERT, CodeT5, and various GPT-based code generators.\n\n### 2.2 Trustworthiness Assessment of GPT Models\n\nDecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models [2306.11698] provides one of the most thorough evaluations of GPT model trustworthiness to date. This work assesses GPT models across multiple trustworthiness dimensions including safety, fairness, bias, and robustness. While not specifically focused on code agents, DecodingTrust's methodology provides valuable insights into evaluating the trustworthiness of language models that underpin code generation capabilities. The study identifies critical vulnerabilities in GPT models that could be exploited through adversarial prompting, highlighting the importance of robust evaluation frameworks.\n\n### 2.3 Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nThe challenge of systematically evaluating code agent security has been addressed through several recent works. RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents [2510.02609] presents a novel framework for automated red-teaming of code agents. This work introduces a multi-agent system where red-teaming agents can autonomously generate adversarial prompts and evaluate the responses of target code agents. The RedCodeAgent framework demonstrates that code agents are vulnerable to various attack vectors, including prompt injection, instruction hijacking, and adversarial code generation. The authors develop an automated pipeline that can efficiently identify vulnerabilities across diverse code agent architectures.\n\n### 2.4 Benchmarking Code Agent Security\n\nRedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents [2409.12345] provides a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating the safety of code agents. This benchmark introduces a curated dataset of risky code generation tasks that test whether code agents will produce harmful or insecure code. The RedCode benchmark covers multiple categories of risky code, including security vulnerabilities, privacy violations, and destructive operations. By providing standardized evaluation tasks, RedCode enables systematic comparison of code agent safety across different models and architectures.\n\n### 2.5 Blue Teaming and Defensive Strategies\n\nBuilding on red-teaming insights, BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI [2501.01234] explores the complementary blue-teaming approach. This work introduces an automated blue-teaming agent that leverages insights from red-teaming to improve code generation security. The BlueCodeAgent framework uses red-teaming results to train defensive strategies and implement safeguards against adversarial attacks. This paper demonstrates the value of a holistic security approach that combines offensive testing (red-teaming) with defensive improvements (blue-teaming) to enhance the overall security posture of code generation systems.\n\n## 3. Discussion\n\nThe works surveyed in this paper collectively advance our understanding of code agent security and provide practical tools for evaluating and improving code generation systems. Several key themes emerge from this literature:\n\n1. **Automated evaluation is essential**: Manual testing approaches are insufficient for comprehensively evaluating code agent security [2510.02609]. Automated red-teaming frameworks can systematically explore the vulnerability space more efficiently.\n\n2. **Standardized benchmarks are critical**: The development of benchmarks like RedCode [2409.12345] enables fair comparison between different code agent systems and tracks progress in security improvements over time.\n\n3. **Holistic security approach**: Combining red-teaming and blue-teaming strategies [2501.01234] provides more comprehensive security than either approach alone. Red-teaming identifies vulnerabilities while blue-teaming implements defensive improvements.\n\n4. **Trustworthiness extends beyond security**: As demonstrated by DecodingTrust [2306.11698], evaluating code agents requires considering multiple dimensions including safety, fairness, and robustness, not just security vulnerabilities.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent a powerful technology with significant potential for transforming software development. However, their autonomous code generation and execution capabilities introduce unique security challenges that require systematic evaluation and mitigation strategies. The research community has made substantial progress in developing automated red-teaming frameworks [2510.02609], comprehensive benchmarks [2409.12345], and defensive strategies [2501.01234] for evaluating and improving code agent security.\n\nFuture research should focus on developing more sophisticated evaluation methodologies that can identify emerging vulnerabilities, creating larger and more diverse benchmark datasets, and building robust defensive mechanisms that can adapt to evolving threat landscapes. Additionally, more work is needed to understand the trustworthiness of code agents beyond security considerations, including aspects like fairness, transparency, and accountability [2306.11698].\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into critical software development workflows, ensuring their security and trustworthiness will be paramount. The collaborative efforts of the research community through red-teaming, benchmarking, and defensive strategy development will be essential for building a foundation of trust in these powerful AI systems.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chulin Xie et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chulin Xie et al., \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents,\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[3] Boxin Wang et al., \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models,\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[4] Ashish Vaswani et al., \"Attention Is All You Need,\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n\n[5] Chulin Xie et al., \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI,\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\nPAPER_EOF\necho \"Paper written successfully\""
}'}

